#[allow(clippy::too_many_lines)]
fn slice_a() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── Rule 1 (category D — dispatch/scheduling, graph-level fusion
        // scheduling): the look-ahead fusion atom — compute a norm +
        // signed-transform cluster EARLY through unrelated in-between
        // nodes, record the downstream tail as precomputed, skip it in
        // the evaluation loop; every safety condition (leaf operands,
        // unit use-counts, the memory-overlap walk, the concurrency
        // gate, the deeper-fusion yield, the width admission) is a
        // load-bearing conjunct, and the fused arithmetic replays the
        // unfused chain's order so bits match. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "look-ahead-fusion-precomputed-skip-overlap-walk".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (graph-level fusion scheduling with a LOOK-AHEAD atom: a fusion computes a node cluster EARLY — ahead of its graph position — through UNRELATED in-between nodes, records the downstream tail in a precomputed set, and the evaluation loop skips the tail when it reaches it; every safety condition the early write demands is a checked conjunct at the match site, never an assumption; category D — dispatch/scheduling, graph-level fusion scheduling): a stock fusion matcher fires on ADJACENT nodes and gives up the moment foreign work sits in the chain — the exact shape this commit attacks is the Bonsai-2 layer, whose every norm feeds a signed Hadamard transform ('cuda: fuse RMS_NORM (+ residual ADD) with the signed Hadamard transform at both norms of every layer'). TWO shapes land in the graph builder's try-fuse. SHAPE 1 — the CONTIGUOUS chain, verbatim: 'Residual ADD -> RMS_NORM -> MUL(weight) -> MUL(Hadamard signs) -> RESHAPE -> MUL_MAT(Hadamard hint): the input of every Bonsai 2 FFN. One launch instead of three (add, norm, signed transform), bit-identical to them.' — matched only at i + 5 < n_nodes with the op tuple (ADD, RMS_NORM, MUL, MUL, RESHAPE, MUL_MAT) and the source wiring checked edge by edge (the norm's src is the add; the weight-mul consumes the norm; the sign-mul consumes the weight-mul; the reshape consumes the sign-mul; the matmul's src[1] is the reshape and carries GGML_HINT_SRC0_IS_HADAMARD via ggml_get_op_params_i32(mm, 1)); admission then demands all-F32 types, same shapes, contiguity everywhere, weight and signs one row wide with ne[0] == ncols, mm->src[0]->ne[0] == ncols && mm->ne[0] == ncols && ggml_nelements(mm) == ggml_nelements(node), the WIDTH ADMISSION ggml_cuda_add_rms_norm_fwht_supported(ncols) — ncols == 1024 || 2048 || 4096, the template widths the kernel family compiled, an ADMISSION and not a heuristic — four explicit data-pointer non-alias checks (weight/signs data against the add's and the mm's buffers), then the two graph-level gates ggml_can_fuse_subgraph(cgraph, i, 6, ops, out_nodes, 2) — the subgraph's TWO outputs are the residual sum (node i) and the mm (node i + 5), so the normalized and weighted rows provably have no consumer outside the window — and ggml_cuda_check_fusion_memory_ranges. One launch (add_rms_norm_fwht_f32<N, true>), return 5: the loop skips the next five nodes. NVIDIA-only; verbatim: 'GGML_CUDA_NORM_FWHT_FUSION=0 disables it; it also stands aside when GGML_CUDA_FWHT_FUSION_PQ2 is set, so that opt-in still quantizes in the transform.' SHAPE 2 — THE LOOK-AHEAD, the novel atom. The layer-start norm has NO adjacent transform: verbatim, the matcher's own comment — 'RMS_NORM -> MUL(weight) ... MUL(Hadamard signs) -> RESHAPE -> MUL_MAT(Hadamard hint), with unrelated nodes in between: the attention input at the start of every Bonsai 2 layer, where the recurrent conv-state reads sit between the norm and the transform, and the normalized row also feeds ssm_alpha/ssm_beta.' The weight-mul IS adjacent (node i + 1); the sign-mul is found by scanning forward k in [i + 2, i + 16] (also bounded by k + 2 < n_nodes so the reshape and mm exist) for a MUL whose src[0] is the weighted row; the fusion then computes the norm, the weighted row and the SIGNED TRANSFORM at the norm's position — out of graph order — writing the transform output into the matmul's buffer many nodes early and the weighted row into the weight-mul's buffer. Verbatim: 'the sign MUL, RESHAPE and transform nodes are recorded as precomputed and skipped when the loop reaches them.' The tail lands in the fwht-q8 context's precomputed set (an unordered_set added in common.cuh — 'nodes a fusion already computed ahead of their position in the graph (ggml_cuda_try_fuse look-ahead); the evaluation loop skips them' — cleared in reset()), and the capture loop's skip is 'if (!...precomputed.empty() && ...precomputed.count(node)) { continue; }' — the empty() short-circuit keeps the hot loop free when no look-ahead fired; the matcher's return 1 covers the norm AND the weight-mul. THE SAFETY CONDITIONS, each load-bearing (the code's ok-chain short-circuits; drop one conjunct and the fusion must not fire): (1) THE LEAF CHECK, verbatim — 'weight and signs are read now: they must be leaves (model tensors), not results of the nodes in between' (op == GGML_OP_NONE on both) — reading them at position i is only sound if nothing between i and j produces them; (2) UNIT USE-COUNTS — get_use_count == 1 on the norm (i), the sign-mul (j) and the reshape (j + 1). The norm's is the sharpest, and the rationale is ADJUDICATED from the launch arguments, not stated upstream: the look-ahead launch passes normed = the weight-mul tensor and dst = the mm tensor and NEVER writes the norm node's own output buffer, so any second reader of the normalized row would read unwritten bytes — use_count(i) == 1 is exactly the license that makes the early launch sound; the tail's counts guarantee the skipped nodes' outputs feed ONLY the fused chain (the signed row the fusion never materializes has no other reader); (3) THE MEMORY-OVERLAP WALK, verbatim — 'mm is written now instead of at j + 2: it must not share memory with the norm's inputs and outputs or with anything the nodes in between read or write' — a byte-range lambda (a0 < b0 + nbytes(b) && b0 < a0 + nbytes(a)) checked mm against x / the weighted row / weight / signs, then every intervening node k in (i + 2, j) against mm — its output AND each src[s] — with the documented exemption 'reading the normalized row (alpha/beta) is fine, it is written here too' (srcs equal to the weight-mul's or the norm's output are skipped; the launch writes those same buffers); (4) THE CONCURRENCY GATE — the arm's outer if requires the stream context's concurrent events EMPTY: verbatim the shape's closing sentence, 'Only taken when none of the nodes in between touches the transform's output buffer (it is written early) and no node stream concurrency is active.' — an early write racing another stream's work is the one hazard the per-node conditions cannot see; (5) the matmul must NOT carry the graph-output flag (an output belongs to its graph position; note the ASYMMETRY with Shape 1, which instead ADMITS the mm as a subgraph output via out_nodes). THE INTER-FUSION PRIORITY YIELD: ggml_cuda_hadamard_feeds_ptq1(cgraph, i_mm) scans forward k in [i_mm + 1, i_mm + 8], tracking reshape aliases of the transform output ('if (t->op == GGML_OP_RESHAPE && t->src[0] == mm) { alias = t; continue; }'), and returns true when a MUL_MAT consumes mm or its alias with a PTQ1_0 src[0]; BOTH shapes refuse when it fires, because — verbatim — 'ggml_cuda_try_fwht_q8 fuses those transforms with their q8_1 quantization by default, so the norm + transform fusions leave them to it': a fusion ladder is a PRIORITY ORDER over shared nodes, the deeper fusion winning, never two independent peepholes racing. BIT IDENTITY BY ORDERING REPLAY: verbatim (norm.cu) — 'Same arithmetic in the same order as add_rms_norm_f32<1024> (or rms_norm_f32<1024> with the multiply) followed by fwht_cuda_block with signs, so the result is bit-identical.' and 'thread tid holds elements tid + i*1024 for both the norm loop and the butterfly, and the butterfly stages run in ascending stride order whatever the block width (ggml_cuda_fwht_block_butterfly).' — the fused kernel re-executes the unfused chain's per-element expression order (scale*x -> *weight -> *fwht_scale -> *=signs, the transform's own 1/sqrtf(ncols) folded) and shares ONE butterfly transcription with the standalone transform kernel, so identity holds by construction and is verified against the unfused chains — never asserted between two fused variants, never a tolerance band. The test file adds two WHOLE-GRAPH families (run_whole_graph): the add+norm+transform family at n in {1024, 2048, 4096} x tokens {1, 4, 8, 33} with BOTH fused outputs checked ('The residual sum is also used by the final ADD'), and the look-ahead family at n in {1024, 4096} whose in-between work includes a SECOND reader of the normalized row (a matmul + scale arm) and whose builder forces the interleaving by operand order ('the first operand's subtree comes first in node order, so other lands between the norm and the transform') — the fixture shape any port needs to exercise the window at all. FALLBACK COMPOSITION is part of the contract, verbatim (commit message): 'When it does not fire, the ADD+RMS_NORM+MUL fusion from #209 handles those nodes as before.' — the new arms join the incumbent matcher ladder by composition, never by replacement (#209's aliasing-legality rider lives on fuse-add-rms-norm, popcorn). MEASURED (source-reported, H200): '9B plain decode +4.8-5.4%, MTP3 +1.2-1.7%, DFlash7 +1.3-2.5%; 27B neutral. Validated together with #383 (GGML_CUDA_GDN_FUSION on and off): greedy identical, MUL_MAT 1666/1666, both fusions fire.' OUR-BOX CORRECTION (our league's measurement, never the source's): the riir-ai Bench 1000 bout (2026-10-25, 4090 sm_89 vs the scored tag prism-b10819-909f0ca carrying this commit) measured the opponent +2.11% decode with the #393 host-cut + #391 Hadamard fusion named — their 27B-neutral datum did not hold at our geometry; opponent numbers are PER-GEOMETRY, never portable bars. DECISION CONTENT: (a) the look-ahead is a graph-order violation licensed by a CONJUNCTIVE condition set — leaves, unit use-counts, the overlap walk, empty concurrent events, non-output mm, the deeper-fusion yield — every conjunct checked at the match, the ok-chain short-circuiting exactly like the rule; (b) unit use-counts are the early-write LIFETIME proof (the launch never writes the norm's own buffer; the skipped tail's outputs must have no reader beyond the fused chain); (c) the overlap walk is the early output's ALIASING proof, priced over the whole skipped window in BOTH directions (node outputs AND each src) with the write-it-myself exemption for the rows the launch itself produces; (d) the yield preserves the ladder's priority order — scan forward through reshape aliases for the quantizing consumer and STAND ASIDE, plus the env-keyed stand-aside for the opt-in quantizing path; (e) bit identity is an ORDERING REPLAY (same expression order per element, ascending stride stages, one shared butterfly transcription) verified against the unfused chains; (f) fallback composition — when the new arm declines, the incumbent #209 shape still owns the nodes; (g) the width admission is the compiled template set (1024/2048/4096), every other width keeps the composed chain, and the kill switch plus the eval-loop empty() guard keep the decline path byte-free. CLASS SIGNATURE: a linear node-list graph evaluation loop with per-node use-counts and byte-range buffer identity available at match time, a fusion candidate cluster whose consumers sit behind UNRELATED nodes, and a scheduler able to compute early, register downstream nodes as covered, and skip them at their position. REGIME: norm-to-transform pairs interleaved with recurrent-state reads (GDN/SSM layer starts); decode-shaped graphs where launch count dominates; builders with alias-checkable allocators. ANTI-REGIME: concurrent stream events racing the early write; a skipped node with use-count > 1; an in-between node whose buffers overlap the early output; weights/signs computed mid-graph (non-leaves); widths outside the compiled set; transforms the deeper quantizing fusion owns; graphs where the transform's matmul is the pinned output. KINSHIP (do not double-count): rht-epilogue-fusion-contract (entries_gpu_18; Batch 105 + the Batch-251 fold-site-per-producer rider) owns WHERE a Hadamard may fuse into a GEMM EPILOGUE (split-K illegality, bias demotion) — an epilogue fusion never leaves its producer's launch; THIS owns the GRAPH-LEVEL look-ahead that bridges NON-adjacent nodes across foreign work — the axis this rule adds. reduction-restructure-needs-source-owned-comparator (entries_gpu_09, B95) is the bit-identity ORACLE axis; its Batch-251 rider (SINGLE-TRANSCRIPTION: the shared in-register block butterfly between the standalone transform kernel and the fused writer, byte-parity per fold site) is exactly the construction this commit's kernel leans on — cite it for the oracle law, this for the graph-scheduling atom. verify-window-prework-loads-before-stores (entries_gpu_52, B182) is WITHIN-round prework ordering (all loads before any store inside ONE kernel); THIS is cross-NODE ordering (a cluster computed before its graph position) — adjacent axis, different tier. pipelined-token-slot-ping-pong-submit-ahead (entries_gpu_18, B105) is DEVICE SUBMIT-AHEAD of the next STEP (the step loop is the object); THIS reorders nodes INSIDE one graph. fuse-add-rms-norm (entries_popcorn, Batch 9 + its Batch-280 #209 rider) is the fallback shape this composes with — do not re-own the ADD+RMS_NORM+MUL admission or its aliasing carve-out here. gate-activation-precompute-pass-token-threshold-gated (entries_gpu_105, B251) is the grep disambiguation: a precompute PASS hoisting redundant per-(token, head) transcendentals — not a graph-position skip set. GREP EVIDENCE: 'look.?ahead' -> TWO hits, neither an owner (serving-raises-only-behind-strict-flag entries_gpu_19 — the MTPLX lookahead engagement assert, a measurement-law mention; pipelined-token-slot-ping-pong-submit-ahead entries_gpu_18 — 'deeper lookahead needs a slot per in-flight step', device submit-ahead); 'precomputed' -> the table/cache/pass classes only (xor-swizzle-smem-static-index-tables, gate-activation-precompute-pass-token-threshold-gated, integer-mma-span-epilogue-dequant's precomputed group sums, hot-swap-weights-preserve-autotune-caches); 'computed ahead|ahead of (its|their) position' -> ZERO over the corpus at claim time; no owner of graph-level look-ahead fusion or of a precomputed-node skip set. NotBenchable here (llama.cpp CUDA graph-builder sources; the corpus serves no CUDA graph validator — the matcher conjuncts, the overlap walk, the skip and the ordering replay are CPU-testable and ARE the fixtures; the H200 figures are source-reported provenance and the 4090 correction is our league bout's — neither is an adoption bar). Source: PrismML/llama.cpp commit e75baeeb24 (#391, bri-prism, 2026-10-24, MIT lineage) — ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu (both fusion shapes, ggml_cuda_hadamard_feeds_ptq1, the capture-loop skip), common.cuh (the precomputed set on the fwht-q8 context, cleared in reset()), norm.cu + norm.cuh (add_rms_norm_fwht_f32<N, has_add>, ggml_cuda_add_rms_norm_fwht_supported), tests/test-backend-ops.cpp (test_add_rms_norm_fwht + test_rms_norm_fwht_lookahead, whole-graph, both outputs checked). Batch 283 / Plan 287 / Research 320.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// ref_shape: the evaluation loop launches each node in order, and\n// the fusion matcher only inspects a FIXED ADJACENT window. The\n// layer-start shape it wants to fuse — norm, weight-mul, then the\n// sign-mul — has unrelated recurrent-state reads sitting in between,\n// so the window never matches and three launches run where one could.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nenum Op {\n    Norm,\n    MulWeight,\n    MulSign,\n    Other, // unrelated work (recurrent state reads) between the arms\n}\n\nstruct Node {\n    op: Op,\n}\n\n/// the naive matcher: a fixed adjacent window [i, i+2]. Any unrelated\n/// node in between breaks the chain and the matcher gives up.\nfn try_fuse_adjacent(graph: &[Node], i: usize) -> Option<usize> {\n    let w = graph.get(i..i + 3)?;\n    match (w[0].op, w[1].op, w[2].op) {\n        (Op::Norm, Op::MulWeight, Op::MulSign) => Some(2), // fuse 3 nodes\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n\n/// the naive evaluation loop: one launch per node (or per matched\n/// window), strictly in graph order.\nfn evaluate_naive(graph: &[Node]) -> u32 {\n    let mut launches = 0;\n    let mut i = 0;\n    while i < graph.len() {\n        match try_fuse_adjacent(graph, i) {\n            Some(extra) => {\n                launches += 1; // one launch for the matched window\n                i += extra + 1;\n            }\n            None => {\n                launches += 1; // this node, alone\n                i += 1;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    launches\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn unrelated_nodes_break_the_adjacent_window() {\n    // the layer-start shape: the sign-mul sits at i+4, two unrelated\n    // state reads after the weight-mul\n    let graph = vec![\n        Node { op: Op::Norm },\n        Node { op: Op::MulWeight },\n        Node { op: Op::Other },\n        Node { op: Op::Other },\n        Node { op: Op::MulSign },\n        Node { op: Op::Other },\n    ];\n    // the matcher never fires: every window it probes ends before the\n    // sign-mul, because the state reads sit in between\n    assert_eq!(try_fuse_adjacent(&graph, 0), None);\n    assert_eq!(try_fuse_adjacent(&graph, 1), None);\n    // so every node launches alone: 6 launches for a graph that holds\n    // one norm+weight+sign cluster's work\n    assert_eq!(evaluate_naive(&graph), 6);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_adjacent_form_still_fires_where_nothing_sits_between() {\n    let graph = vec![\n        Node { op: Op::Norm },\n        Node { op: Op::MulWeight },\n        Node { op: Op::MulSign },\n        Node { op: Op::Other },\n    ];\n    assert_eq!(try_fuse_adjacent(&graph, 0), Some(2));\n    // the fused window (1 launch) + the trailing node (1) = 2\n    assert_eq!(evaluate_naive(&graph), 2);\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// opt_pattern: the graph-level look-ahead fusion on a small node vec.\n// (1) the CONTIGUOUS chain (add, norm, weight-mul, sign-mul, reshape,\n// matmul) fuses to ONE launch; (2) the LOOK-AHEAD finds the sign-mul\n// through unrelated state reads, computes norm + weighted row +\n// transform EARLY, records the tail in a precomputed set, and the\n// evaluation loop skips the tail when it reaches it. Every safety\n// condition is load-bearing: leaf operands, unit use-counts, the\n// memory-overlap walk over the skipped window, no stream concurrency,\n// the deeper-fusion yield, the width admission. The fused arithmetic\n// replays the unfused chain's order, so bits match.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nuse std::collections::HashSet;\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nenum Op {\n    Add,\n    Norm,\n    MulWeight,\n    MulSign,\n    Reshape,\n    MatMul,\n    State,  // unrelated recurrent work (conv-state / gate reads)\n    Consume, // a final combiner reading the arms\n}\n\n/// a buffer = a byte range in one flat arena: aliasing is range overlap.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nstruct Buf {\n    off: usize,\n    len: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Buf {\n    fn overlaps(self, o: Buf) -> bool {\n        // the upstream lambda's shape:\n        // a0 < b0 + nbytes(b) && b0 < a0 + nbytes(a)\n        self.off < o.off + o.len && o.off < self.off + self.len\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\nstruct Src {\n    buf: Buf,\n    leaf: bool, // a model tensor, never a computed result\n}\n\nstruct Node {\n    op: Op,\n    out: Buf,\n    srcs: Vec<Src>,\n    hadamard: bool,   // matmul: src1 is Hadamard-structured (the hint)\n    use_count: usize, // later readers of this node's output\n    ptq_feed: bool,   // feeds the deeper quantizing fusion\n    is_output: bool,  // pinned graph output\n}\n\nfn b(off: usize) -> Buf {\n    Buf { off, len: 4 }\n}\n\nfn n(op: Op, out: Buf, srcs: Vec<(Buf, bool)>) -> Node {\n    Node {\n        op,\n        out,\n        srcs: srcs.into_iter().map(|(buf, leaf)| Src { buf, leaf }).collect(),\n        hadamard: false,\n        use_count: 1,\n        ptq_feed: false,\n        is_output: false,\n    }\n}\n\n/// the width admission: the compiled template widths, nothing else.\nfn transform_width_admitted(ncols: usize) -> bool {\n    matches!(ncols, 1024 | 2048 | 4096)\n}\n\n/// does the transform output at i_mm feed a quantizing matmul within a\n/// few nodes, directly or through a reshape alias? The deeper fusion\n/// (transform + quantize) owns that transform — this fusion yields.\nfn hadamard_feeds_ptq(graph: &[Node], i_mm: usize) -> bool {\n    let mm_out = graph[i_mm].out;\n    let mut alias = mm_out;\n    for k in (i_mm + 1)..graph.len().min(i_mm + 9) {\n        let t = &graph[k];\n        if t.op == Op::Reshape && t.srcs.iter().any(|s| s.buf == alias) {\n            alias = t.out;\n            continue;\n        }\n        if t.op == Op::MatMul\n            && t.ptq_feed\n            && t.srcs.len() == 2\n            && (t.srcs[1].buf == mm_out || t.srcs[1].buf == alias)\n        {\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n    false\n}\n\n/// (1) the contiguous six-node chain: returns the upstream-style skip\n/// count (nodes consumed beyond i) when it fires.\nfn try_fuse_chain(graph: &[Node], i: usize, ncols: usize, enabled: bool) -> Option<usize> {\n    if !enabled || i + 5 >= graph.len() || !transform_width_admitted(ncols) {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let w = &graph[i..i + 6];\n    let ops_ok = matches!(\n        (w[0].op, w[1].op, w[2].op, w[3].op, w[4].op, w[5].op),\n        (Op::Add, Op::Norm, Op::MulWeight, Op::MulSign, Op::Reshape, Op::MatMul)\n    );\n    // the chain's wiring, edge by edge\n    let wire_ok = w[1].srcs.first().map(|s| s.buf) == Some(w[0].out)\n        && w[2].srcs.first().map(|s| s.buf) == Some(w[1].out)\n        && w[3].srcs.first().map(|s| s.buf) == Some(w[2].out)\n        && w[4].srcs.first().map(|s| s.buf) == Some(w[3].out)\n        && w[5].srcs.get(1).map(|s| s.buf) == Some(w[4].out)\n        && w[5].hadamard;\n    // the intermediates (norm, weighted row, signed row) have no\n    // consumers outside the fused subgraph; the two NAMED outputs (the\n    // residual sum, the mm) may be consumed elsewhere\n    let counts_ok = w[1].use_count == 1 && w[2].use_count == 1 && w[3].use_count == 1;\n    // the deeper quantizing fusion owns a transform it can reach\n    let yield_ok = !hadamard_feeds_ptq(graph, i + 5);\n    (ops_ok && wire_ok && counts_ok && yield_ok).then_some(5)\n}\n\n/// (2) the look-ahead: norm at i, weight-mul at i+1, then ANY nodes,\n/// then the sign-mul of the same weighted row within k <= i + 16.\n/// Returns (skip count, downstream node indices to precompute).\nfn try_fuse_lookahead(\n    graph: &[Node],\n    i: usize,\n    ncols: usize,\n    enabled: bool,\n    concurrency_active: bool,\n) -> Option<(usize, Vec<usize>)> {\n    if !enabled || i + 4 >= graph.len() || !transform_width_admitted(ncols) {\n        return None;\n    }\n    // the transform output is written early: no other stream may race it\n    if concurrency_active {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let norm = &graph[i];\n    if norm.op != Op::Norm || norm.use_count != 1 {\n        return None; // the fused launch never writes the norm's own\n                     // output buffer, so it must have exactly one\n                     // consumer (the weight-mul the launch fills)\n    }\n    let mul_w = &graph[i + 1];\n    if mul_w.op != Op::MulWeight {\n        return None; // the weight-mul is ADJACENT; the gap is after it\n    }\n    // the look-ahead window: find the sign-mul of the same weighted row\n    let mut j = None;\n    for k in (i + 2)..graph.len().min(i + 17) {\n        if graph[k].op == Op::MulSign {\n            j = Some(k);\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    let j = j?;\n    if j + 2 >= graph.len() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let (mul_s, reshape, mm) = (&graph[j], &graph[j + 1], &graph[j + 2]);\n    if reshape.op != Op::Reshape\n        || reshape.srcs.first().map(|s| s.buf) != Some(mul_s.out)\n        || mm.op != Op::MatMul\n        || mm.srcs.get(1).map(|s| s.buf) != Some(reshape.out)\n        || !mm.hadamard\n        || mm.is_output\n    {\n        return None;\n    }\n    // unit use-counts on the skipped tail: the signed row and its\n    // reshape alias must feed exactly the fused chain\n    if mul_s.use_count != 1 || reshape.use_count != 1 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    // the deeper quantizing fusion wins the shared nodes\n    if hadamard_feeds_ptq(graph, j + 2) {\n        return None;\n    }\n    // LEAF CHECK: weight and signs are read NOW, at the norm's\n    // position, so they must be leaves (model tensors), never results\n    // of the nodes in between\n    if mul_w.srcs.get(1).map(|s| s.leaf) != Some(true)\n        || mul_s.srcs.get(1).map(|s| s.leaf) != Some(true)\n    {\n        return None;\n    }\n    // MEMORY-OVERLAP WALK: mm is written early, so it must not share\n    // memory with the norm's inputs and outputs, the weighted row, or\n    // anything the nodes in between read or write\n    let mm_buf = mm.out;\n    let mut ok = norm\n        .srcs\n        .iter()\n        .chain(mul_w.srcs.iter())\n        .chain(mul_s.srcs.iter())\n        .all(|s| !mm_buf.overlaps(s.buf))\n        && !mm_buf.overlaps(norm.out)\n        && !mm_buf.overlaps(mul_w.out);\n    for t in &graph[i + 2..j] {\n        if !ok {\n            break;\n        }\n        ok &= !mm_buf.overlaps(t.out);\n        for s in &t.srcs {\n            // reading the normalized row / the weighted row is fine:\n            // the fused launch writes those same buffers\n            if s.buf == norm.out || s.buf == mul_w.out {\n                continue;\n            }\n            ok &= !mm_buf.overlaps(s.buf);\n        }\n    }\n    ok.then(|| (1, vec![j, j + 1, j + 2]))\n}\n\n/// the evaluation loop: launches each node (or a fused cluster) in\n/// order, skipping nodes a look-ahead fusion already computed.\nfn evaluate(\n    graph: &[Node],\n    ncols: usize,\n    enabled: bool,\n    concurrency_active: bool,\n) -> (u32, Vec<usize>) {\n    let mut precomputed: HashSet<usize> = HashSet::new();\n    let mut launched: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\n    let mut launches = 0;\n    let mut i = 0;\n    while i < graph.len() {\n        // computed ahead of its position by a look-ahead fusion: skipped\n        // when the loop reaches it — no launch\n        if precomputed.contains(&i) {\n            i += 1;\n            continue;\n        }\n        if let Some(extra) = try_fuse_chain(graph, i, ncols, enabled) {\n            launches += 1;\n            launched.push(i);\n            i += extra + 1; // the launch covered i .. i + extra\n            continue;\n        }\n        if let Some((extra, tail)) = try_fuse_lookahead(graph, i, ncols, enabled, concurrency_active)\n        {\n            launches += 1;\n            launched.push(i);\n            precomputed.extend(tail); // the tail is skipped at its position\n            i += extra + 1; // the launch covered the norm AND the weighted row\n            continue;\n        }\n        launches += 1;\n        launched.push(i);\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    (launches, launched)\n}\n\n/// the layer-start graph: norm, weight-mul, two unrelated state reads\n/// (one of them a SECOND reader of the normalized row), then the\n/// sign-mul, reshape, hadamard matmul, and a final combiner.\nfn look_graph() -> Vec<Node> {\n    vec![\n        n(Op::Norm, b(15), vec![(b(0), true)]),                       // 0\n        n(Op::MulWeight, b(20), vec![(b(15), false), (b(90), true)]), // 1\n        // 2: ssm-ish read, and a SECOND reader of the normalized row\n        n(Op::State, b(35), vec![(b(15), false), (b(60), true)]),     // 2\n        n(Op::State, b(40), vec![(b(65), true)]),                     // 3: conv-state-ish\n        n(Op::MulSign, b(25), vec![(b(20), false), (b(95), true)]),   // 4\n        n(Op::Reshape, b(25), vec![(b(25), false)]),                  // 5\n        {\n            let mut m = n(Op::MatMul, b(30), vec![(b(50), true), (b(25), false)]);\n            m.hadamard = true;\n            m // 6\n        },\n        n(Op::Consume, b(45), vec![(b(40), false), (b(30), false)]), // 7: the output\n    ]\n}\n\nfn chain_graph() -> Vec<Node> {\n    vec![\n        {\n            let mut a = n(Op::Add, b(10), vec![(b(0), true), (b(5), true)]);\n            a.use_count = 2; // the residual sum also feeds the final add\n            a // 0\n        },\n        n(Op::Norm, b(15), vec![(b(10), false)]),                     // 1\n        n(Op::MulWeight, b(20), vec![(b(15), false), (b(90), true)]), // 2\n        n(Op::MulSign, b(25), vec![(b(20), false), (b(95), true)]),   // 3\n        n(Op::Reshape, b(25), vec![(b(25), false)]),                  // 4\n        {\n            let mut m = n(Op::MatMul, b(30), vec![(b(50), true), (b(25), false)]);\n            m.hadamard = true;\n            m // 5\n        },\n        n(Op::Add, b(40), vec![(b(30), false), (b(10), false)]), // 6: mm + residual\n    ]\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_contiguous_chain_fuses_six_nodes_into_one_launch() {\n    let graph = chain_graph();\n    let (launches, launched) = evaluate(&graph, 1024, true, false);\n    assert_eq!(launches, 2); // the fused chain + the final residual add\n    assert_eq!(launched, vec![0, 6]);\n    // the matcher's skip count mirrors the upstream return\n    assert_eq!(try_fuse_chain(&graph, 0, 1024, true), Some(5));\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_residual_sums_second_consumer_does_not_block_the_chain() {\n    let graph = chain_graph();\n    // node 0 (the residual add) feeds the norm AND the final add: the\n    // two NAMED outputs of the subgraph may be consumed elsewhere; only\n    // the intermediates must be consumer-free\n    assert_eq!(graph[0].use_count, 2);\n    assert!(try_fuse_chain(&graph, 0, 1024, true).is_some());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_lookahead_fires_through_unrelated_state_reads_and_skips_the_tail() {\n    let graph = look_graph();\n    let (launches, launched) = evaluate(&graph, 1024, true, false);\n    // one launch at node 0 (covers 0+1), nodes 2 and 3 alone, the tail\n    // 4/5/6 skipped as precomputed, node 7 alone: 4 launches, not 8\n    assert_eq!(launches, 4);\n    assert_eq!(launched, vec![0, 2, 3, 7]);\n    // the matcher returns the tail it recorded for the precomputed set\n    assert_eq!(\n        try_fuse_lookahead(&graph, 0, 1024, true, false),\n        Some((1, vec![4, 5, 6]))\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn reading_the_normalized_row_in_between_is_not_a_refusal() {\n    // node 2 reads the normalized row: the fused launch writes that\n    // same buffer itself, so the overlap walk exempts it\n    let graph = look_graph();\n    assert_eq!(graph[2].srcs[0].buf, graph[0].out);\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&graph, 0, 1024, true, false).is_some());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn an_intervening_node_aliasing_the_early_output_refuses() {\n    // an in-between node's OUTPUT overlapping mm: it would read or\n    // clobber the bytes the early write puts there\n    let mut g = look_graph();\n    g[2].out = g[6].out;\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&g, 0, 1024, true, false).is_none());\n    // the src-side half: an in-between node READS a buffer overlapping mm\n    let mut g2 = look_graph();\n    g2[3].srcs[0].buf = g2[6].out;\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&g2, 0, 1024, true, false).is_none());\n    // and the direct half: the norm's own output overlapping mm\n    let mut g3 = look_graph();\n    g3[0].out = g3[6].out;\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&g3, 0, 1024, true, false).is_none());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn a_non_leaf_sign_operand_refuses() {\n    // the signs operand is the result of an in-between node: it cannot\n    // be read at the norm's position (it does not exist yet there)\n    let mut g = look_graph();\n    g[4].srcs[1].leaf = false;\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&g, 0, 1024, true, false).is_none());\n    // same for the weight operand of the adjacent mul\n    let mut g2 = look_graph();\n    g2[1].srcs[1].leaf = false;\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&g2, 0, 1024, true, false).is_none());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn unit_use_counts_are_the_early_write_lifetime_proof() {\n    // the fused launch never writes the norm's own output buffer, so a\n    // second reader of it would read unwritten bytes\n    let mut g = look_graph();\n    g[0].use_count = 2;\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&g, 0, 1024, true, false).is_none());\n    // a second consumer of the signed row: the fusion never\n    // materializes it, so that reader would read garbage\n    let mut g2 = look_graph();\n    g2[4].use_count = 2;\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&g2, 0, 1024, true, false).is_none());\n    // a second consumer of the reshape alias\n    let mut g3 = look_graph();\n    g3[5].use_count = 2;\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&g3, 0, 1024, true, false).is_none());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_deeper_quantizing_fusion_wins_the_transform() {\n    // the transform output feeds (through a reshape alias) a matmul\n    // whose activation is quantized: the deeper fusion owns it, this\n    // fusion stands aside\n    let mut g = look_graph();\n    g.push(n(Op::Reshape, b(70), vec![(b(30), false)])); // 7: alias of mm\n    g.push({\n        let mut m = n(Op::MatMul, b(80), vec![(b(75), true), (b(70), false)]);\n        m.ptq_feed = true;\n        m // 8\n    });\n    assert!(hadamard_feeds_ptq(&g, 6));\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&g, 0, 1024, true, false).is_none());\n    // control: the same tail without the quantizing consumer fires\n    assert!(!hadamard_feeds_ptq(&look_graph(), 6));\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn stream_concurrency_blocks_the_early_write() {\n    let graph = look_graph();\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&graph, 0, 1024, true, false).is_some());\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&graph, 0, 1024, true, true).is_none());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_width_admission_and_the_kill_switch_gate_both_arms() {\n    let look = look_graph();\n    let chain = chain_graph();\n    // width admission: only the compiled template widths\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&look, 0, 1024, true, false).is_some());\n    assert!(try_fuse_lookahead(&look, 0, 512, true, false).is_none());\n    assert!(try_fuse_chain(&chain, 0, 4096, true).is_some());\n    assert!(try_fuse_chain(&chain, 0, 8192, true).is_none());\n    // the kill switch: disabled = the composed chain, every node alone\n    assert_eq!(evaluate(&look, 1024, false, false).0, 8);\n    assert_eq!(evaluate(&chain, 1024, false, false).0, 7);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_fused_arithmetic_replays_the_unfused_order_bit_identically() {\n    let x = [0.37f32, -1.2, 0.9, 0.05];\n    let weight = [1.1f32, 0.8, 1.3, 0.95];\n    let signs = [1.0f32, -1.0, 1.0, -1.0];\n    let eps = 1e-6f32;\n    let a = unfused_chain(&x, &weight, &signs, eps);\n    let c = fused_norm_weight_sign_fwht(&x, &weight, &signs, eps);\n    assert_eq!(a.map(f32::to_bits), c.map(f32::to_bits));\n}\n\n/// the unfused chain: norm pass, weight pass, sign pass, butterfly —\n/// three launches' worth of arithmetic.\nfn unfused_chain(x: &[f32; 4], weight: &[f32; 4], signs: &[f32; 4], eps: f32) -> [f32; 4] {\n    // pass 1: the norm\n    let mut ss = 0.0f32;\n    for v in x {\n        ss += v * v;\n    }\n    let scale = 1.0 / (ss / 4.0 + eps).sqrt();\n    let mut normed = [0.0f32; 4];\n    for i in 0..4 {\n        normed[i] = scale * x[i];\n    }\n    // pass 2: the weight mul\n    let mut weighted = [0.0f32; 4];\n    for i in 0..4 {\n        weighted[i] = normed[i] * weight[i];\n    }\n    // pass 3: the transform's own 1/sqrt(n), the signs, the butterfly\n    let fwht_scale = 1.0 / (4.0f32).sqrt();\n    let mut reg = [0.0f32; 4];\n    for i in 0..4 {\n        let mut r = weighted[i] * fwht_scale;\n        r *= signs[i];\n        reg[i] = r;\n    }\n    fwht4(&mut reg);\n    reg\n}\n\n/// the fused launch: ONE pass — but the per-element expression order is\n/// the unfused chain's: (scale * x) * weight, then * fwht_scale, then\n/// *= sign. The norm loop and the butterfly hold the SAME element.\nfn fused_norm_weight_sign_fwht(x: &[f32; 4], weight: &[f32; 4], signs: &[f32; 4], eps: f32) -> [f32; 4] {\n    let mut ss = 0.0f32;\n    for v in x {\n        ss += v * v;\n    }\n    let scale = 1.0 / (ss / 4.0 + eps).sqrt();\n    let fwht_scale = 1.0 / (4.0f32).sqrt();\n    let mut reg = [0.0f32; 4];\n    for i in 0..4 {\n        let mut r = scale * x[i];\n        r = r * weight[i];\n        r = r * fwht_scale;\n        r *= signs[i];\n        reg[i] = r;\n    }\n    fwht4(&mut reg);\n    reg\n}\n\n/// the butterfly, stages in ASCENDING stride order (1, then 2) — the\n/// same order whatever the block width.\nfn fwht4(v: &mut [f32; 4]) {\n    let mut len = 1;\n    while len < 4 {\n        for start in (0..4).step_by(len * 2) {\n            for j in start..start + len {\n                let u = v[j];\n                let t = v[j + len];\n                v[j] = u + t;\n                v[j + len] = u - t;\n            }\n        }\n        len *= 2;\n    }\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}

pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    let v = slice_a();
    debug_assert_eq!(v.len(), 1, "batch 283: 1 own kernel_opt rule");
    v
}
